# For which domains do effect-size estimates tend to be larger in original studies than in replications?

Data: 86 original/replication pairs (meta-regression joined file). Domain = discipline (Social 34, Cognitive 34,
Economics 18); project.y (rpp 68 / ee 18) as coarser secondary grouping. Discipline is perfectly nested in project
(Economics == ee), so Economics cannot be separated from the ee protocol/era.

## Hypothesis (as-observed, descriptive)
Original effect sizes exceed replication effect sizes in **every** domain: originals > replications in 73/86 pairs
(84.9%), mean Fisher-z gap fiso-fisr = 0.262 (paired t p=3.5e-12). The domain question is therefore about the SIZE
of the gap, and the answer depends on how "larger" is measured:

1. **Relative / proportional shrinkage (relative_es = rr/ro, |rr|/|ro|, sign reversals, replication success):**
   **Social psychology shows the largest original-minus-replication gap**, intermediate for cognitive psychology,
   smallest for experimental economics. mean relative_es 0.28 (Social) vs 0.51 (Cognitive) vs 0.66 (Economics);
   medians 0.13 / 0.65 / 0.70; |rr|/|ro| 0.41 / 0.64 / 0.72; sign reversals 29.4% / 14.7% / 16.7%; replication
   success 20.6% / 47.1% / 61.1%. Social ranks 1st in 8 of the 12 tabulated framings (all four relative-ES framings, frac|rr|<|ro|,
   sign-reversal rate, replication-failure rate, and the FE-weighted D_z); the relative ordering
   Social > Cognitive > Economics in shrinkage is unchanged in every robustness variant tested
   (artifact-row fix, po<=0.05 only, excluding sign reversals, rr floored at 0). Social vs Economics difference in mean relative_es = -0.378 (95% bootstrap CI -0.71 to -0.05, p=0.022;
   Mann-Whitney p=0.019); Social vs Cognitive = -0.231 (CI -0.48 to +0.04, MW p=0.015, bootstrap p=0.089) - the
   medians (0.13 vs 0.65) separate more cleanly than the means, which are pulled by outliers.

2. **Signed absolute Fisher-z gap (D_z = fiso - fisr):** the ordering changes and is *not* statistically
   distinguishable. Unweighted means: Cognitive 0.289 > Economics 0.266 > Social 0.233. Random-effects pooled:
   Cognitive 0.234 [0.142,0.326] ~ Social 0.222 [0.150,0.295] > Economics 0.117 [0.010,0.223]. Between-domain
   moderator test with random effects: Q=2.91, df=2, p=0.234 (FE-weighted version Q=24.9, p<0.0001, driven entirely
   by the ee fixed-effect collapse described below).

So the substantive claim is: **originals overstate replications most severely, relatively, in social psychology
(social/attitude/personality-type effects), less in cognitive psychology, and least in experimental economics;
measured as an absolute Fisher-z difference the gap is roughly comparable across domains (cognitive numerically
largest) and no domain difference reaches significance.**

## Why the two metrics disagree (mechanism, as-observed)
Social-psychology originals have the smallest baseline effects (mean ro 0.346 vs 0.533 cognitive), so an equal
proportional shrinkage produces a smaller absolute Fisher-z gap. Exploratory meta-regression shows the absolute gap
is driven by selection-type features rather than domain: se_fiso b=+1.29 (p<0.0001) and original effect size fiso
b=+0.45 (p<0.0001); with original ES included, domain coefficients become non-significant (Social +0.032 p=0.60,
Economics -0.079 p=0.28). Larger original effects and less precise originals shrink most - the winner's-curse /
significance-selection pattern. Within-stratum tables show Social<Cognitive on relative_es in most
original-N/ro/replication-N strata, and era is fully confounded with project (34/34 social and 33/34 cognitive
papers published in 2008, one cognitive paper in 2015; all 18 economics papers 2011-2014), so any
psychology-vs-economics contrast is also a 4-6 year era/protocol contrast.

## Headline weighting diagnostic (ee, n=18)
D_z for ee by estimator: unweighted 0.266 [0.084,0.447]; RE(DL) 0.129 [0.011,0.246]; RE(REML) 0.117 [0.010,0.223];
fixed-effect/inv-variance **0.013 [-0.052,0.078] (indistinguishable from zero)**. Cause: 3 studies carry 60.4% of
inverse-variance weight (de Clippel et al. AER 2014 alone 43.1%, D_z=-0.155, n_orig=790/n_repl=780), versus a 15.0%
top-3 weight share in rpp. The ee ordering is therefore NOT stable to weighting: unweighted and RE make ee the
smallest-gap domain, FE makes the ee gap vanish entirely, while the relative metric (relative_es 0.657, highest of
all domains, i.e. smallest shrinkage) is stable. Ordering stable across weighting: Social and Cognitive are similar
and Economics/ee has the smallest proportional shrinkage. Ordering NOT stable: which of Social/Cognitive has the
largest absolute D_z, and whether the ee absolute gap is "small" (RE) or "absent" (FE).

## Robustness / stability
- Leave-one-out (RE pooled D_z): Social +0.223 (LOO +0.207..+0.241), Cognitive +0.234 (+0.212..+0.250),
  Economics +0.117 (+0.092..+0.141), rpp +0.227 (+0.217..+0.237), ee +0.117 (+0.092..+0.141) - no single study
  drives any domain estimate (max LOO swing 0.049 for ee).
- Jackknife of contrasts: Social-Cognitive -0.017 [-0.038,+0.009] (sign flips, i.e. genuinely null);
  Social-Economics +0.099 [+0.057,+0.129]; Cognitive-Economics +0.116 [+0.071,+0.137]; rpp-ee +0.105 [+0.062,+0.125]
  - the ee gap is consistently smaller than psychology but its RE CI includes 0 (p=0.10-0.15).
- Within each domain originals > replications (paired t / Wilcoxon / sign-flip permutation p<=0.0004; binomial
  |rr|<|ro| p<=0.004), so the "originals larger" statement holds domain by domain.
- Excluding sign-reversed pairs (H4) or flooring rr at 0 (H5) reduces all D_z but leaves the relative ordering
  Social<Cognitive<Economics unchanged; Social then ties for the largest RE D_z (0.165 vs Cognitive 0.158).
- The Eitam artifact row (stored relative_es=-0.472, recomputed rr/ro=+0.472) raises Social mean relative_es from
  0.278 to 0.306 and median from 0.131 to 0.184; rank order unchanged in every variant.
- Limitations: `aggregated` and `lab_id` are entirely empty so multi-effect aggregation/lab clustering cannot be
  checked or modelled; 86 rows is a selected, significance-filtered sample of both projects; Economics==ee, so
  domain, replication protocol, era (median 2012 vs 2008) and replication power are inseparable; independence of
  original and replication assumed for var(fiso-fisr)=se_fiso^2+se_fisr^2.
